+
+## 3a. Seed-first analysis (REQUIRED)
+
+Run the six-question checklist (`KB § GUIDES/seed-first-design.md § The seed-first checklist`):
+
+1. **Is the contract identical for every product?** **NO** — AdConnect uniquely uses custom-JWT for its domain auth (because B2B distributors log in via email/password, not Supabase SSO). Every other product uses Supabase. The seed already provides Supabase auth; AdConnect's local `auth_deps.py` is the *correct* placement for the deviation.
+2. **Is the data source product-specific?** **YES** — the in-memory `data/store.users` and the JWT secret in `app/config` are AdConnect-only. Brand admin / customer roles are AdConnect-only.
+3. **Is the placement product-specific?** **YES** — domain-bound (custom JWT for B2B distributor login).
+4. **Is the visibility / permission rule the same?** **NO** — AdConnect uses `role in ("admin", "customer")` from JWT claims; other products use Supabase user_metadata.role / SSO resolution.
+5. **Does the seam already exist in seed?** **PARTIALLY** — the seed has `make_get_current_user` (Supabase JWT validator) and `make_require_role` (role gate factory). Both assume Supabase as the validator. AdConnect's `decode_token` from `app/security` is the equivalent for HS256-self-minted tokens. **There is NO seed seam for "custom-JWT auth"** — and Phase 0 sees N=1 (only AdConnect needs it). **Recurrence rule outcome: no formalization at N=1; revisit if a 2nd custom-JWT product appears.**
+6. **Default-on or opt-in?** **OPT-IN** — most products inherit Supabase auth from the seed; AdConnect is the explicit opt-out.
+
+**Litmus — per-product code count this design requires:**
+
+- [x] **A small section** — AdConnect has ~40 lines of custom-JWT auth in `app/auth_deps.py`. Acceptable because the deviation is product-specific (B2B distributor email/password vs. SSO). Per-product count for OTHER products = 0 (they inherit Supabase from the seed factory).
+
+**Phase plan implications:** §6 phases work in **AdConnect only** — no per-product walk. The seed-side action (if any) is documenting the carve-out in KB so future products with similar needs find the pattern.
